1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:07,468
Welcome to Level Seven of the CMI Smart Money Concept programme: Advanced ICT Concepts.

2
00:00:07,888 --> 00:00:13,648
In this first episode, we study the Power of Three, often shortened to PO3.

3
00:00:14,068 --> 00:00:22,180
By the end, you should be able to define the framework, describe its three phases, and build bullish and bearish models step by step.

4
00:00:22,600 --> 00:00:28,960
We will also study where the model fails, because a framework is only useful when you know its limits.

5
00:00:30,780 --> 00:00:36,180
Level Seven has six original subtopics, and we keep them in this exact order.

6
00:00:36,600 --> 00:00:44,400
Power of Three, AMD, Kill Zones, Daily Bias, Judas Swing, and SMT Divergence.

7
00:00:44,820 --> 00:00:54,348
Each one builds on what you learned in Levels Two to Six: structure, liquidity, institutional zones, displacement, and premium and discount.

8
00:00:54,768 --> 00:01:01,104
Treat every concept here as an analytical framework, not as proof of what institutions are doing.

9
00:01:02,924 --> 00:01:09,116
So what is the Power of Three? At CMI, we define it as a conceptual model of price delivery.

10
00:01:09,536 --> 00:01:16,712
The framework was popularised in modern trading education by the Inner Circle Trader, Michael J. Huddleston.

11
00:01:17,132 --> 00:01:27,308
However, the underlying ideas, such as trading ranges and runs on stops, are much older. Wyckoff wrote about accumulation in the early twentieth century.

12
00:01:27,728 --> 00:01:34,472
PO3 is usually drawn as three phases: accumulation, manipulation and distribution.

13
00:01:34,892 --> 00:01:40,340
It describes one possible sequence. It is not a pattern that every day must produce.

14
00:01:40,760 --> 00:01:46,472
Keep a clear line between what you observe on the chart, and the label you choose to interpret it with.

15
00:01:48,292 --> 00:01:53,476
Let’s build the three phases on a model chart. The gold line is the opening price.

16
00:01:53,896 --> 00:02:00,376
Phase one is accumulation. Price forms a range and stays relatively contained around the open.

17
00:02:00,796 --> 00:02:07,708
Directional progress is limited, and liquidity may build around the obvious range highs and lows.

18
00:02:08,128 --> 00:02:14,728
Phase two is manipulation. Price moves beyond one side of the range, here below the range low.

19
00:02:15,148 --> 00:02:21,796
This may sweep sell-side liquidity resting below the lows, and it moves against the direction that follows.

20
00:02:22,216 --> 00:02:26,224
Traders often call this a false breakout or a liquidity raid.

21
00:02:26,644 --> 00:02:32,092
Phase three is distribution. Price expands directionally away from the range.

22
00:02:32,512 --> 00:02:38,728
Market structure develops, and price may travel toward a liquidity objective, such as an old high.

23
00:02:40,548 --> 00:02:45,492
Two words in this model need careful handling. The first is manipulation.

24
00:02:45,912 --> 00:02:53,424
A sweep through a level is an observable event. Calling it manipulation is an interpretation from the ICT framework.

25
00:02:53,844 --> 00:03:01,308
A sweep alone does not prove deliberate institutional action. Stops are triggered by many participants, and by news.

26
00:03:01,728 --> 00:03:08,304
The second word is distribution. In PO3, it simply means the directional expansion phase.

27
00:03:08,724 --> 00:03:16,860
So in a bullish PO3, distribution moves up. Do not confuse it with Wyckoff Distribution, which is a topping process.

28
00:03:18,680 --> 00:03:24,152
Now the full bullish model, step by step. Step one: mark the opening price.

29
00:03:24,572 --> 00:03:30,356
Step two: price consolidates around the open, rotating above and below it.

30
00:03:30,776 --> 00:03:38,672
Step three: identify the range high and range low. Sell-side liquidity is assumed to rest below the lows.

31
00:03:39,092 --> 00:03:44,468
Step four: price moves below the range low, trading under the opening price.

32
00:03:44,888 --> 00:03:52,040
Step five: this candle visibly trades through the lows. That is a possible sell-side liquidity sweep.

33
00:03:52,460 --> 00:03:57,116
Step six: price fails to continue lower and reclaims the range.

34
00:03:57,536 --> 00:04:03,440
Step seven: bullish displacement. Strong candles leave a fair value gap behind them.

35
00:04:03,860 --> 00:04:10,796
Step eight: a candle closes above the last lower high. That is a possible bullish market structure shift.

36
00:04:11,216 --> 00:04:15,560
Step nine: price expands upward, away from the range.

37
00:04:15,980 --> 00:04:23,780
Step ten: a possible objective is buy-side liquidity above an old high, here at four thousand two hundred and four.

38
00:04:24,200 --> 00:04:33,560
Labelled together: accumulation, manipulation, distribution. Remember, this is one possible bullish sequence, not a guarantee.

39
00:04:35,380 --> 00:04:40,228
The bearish model is the inverse. Step one: mark the opening price.

40
00:04:40,648 --> 00:04:44,824
Step two: accumulation. Price ranges around the open.

41
00:04:45,244 --> 00:04:52,972
Step three: identify the range high and range low. This time we focus on buy-side liquidity above the highs.

42
00:04:53,392 --> 00:04:58,744
Step four: price moves above the range high, trading over the opening price.

43
00:04:59,164 --> 00:05:04,900
Step five: the wick trades through the highs. A possible buy-side liquidity sweep.

44
00:05:05,320 --> 00:05:10,240
Step six: price cannot hold above and returns below the range high.

45
00:05:10,660 --> 00:05:15,604
Step seven: bearish displacement, leaving a bearish fair value gap.

46
00:05:16,024 --> 00:05:22,192
Step eight: a close below the last higher low. A possible bearish market structure shift.

47
00:05:22,612 --> 00:05:25,444
Step nine: price expands downward.

48
00:05:25,864 --> 00:05:31,168
Step ten: a possible objective is sell-side liquidity below an old low.

49
00:05:31,588 --> 00:05:37,132
Accumulation, manipulation, distribution again, with the direction reversed.

50
00:05:38,952 --> 00:05:45,888
The opening price is the anchor of the PO3 model, so we need to be precise about which open we mean.

51
00:05:46,308 --> 00:05:54,108
The daily open is the first price of the trading day under a stated convention. In ICT forex material, that is often New York midnight.

52
00:05:54,528 --> 00:06:01,872
A session open is the first price of a session, such as London, New York, or an FCPO trading session.

53
00:06:02,292 --> 00:06:04,692
A daily PO3 uses the daily open. A session-based PO3 uses the session open.

54
00:06:05,112 --> 00:06:14,760
For FCPO, the day session opens at ten thirty Malaysia time, and the after-hours T plus one session is booked to the next trading day.

55
00:06:15,180 --> 00:06:19,620
So choose one convention, write it on your chart, and keep it consistent.

56
00:06:20,040 --> 00:06:28,416
And remember: the open is a reference. It is not automatic support or resistance, and it is not the same as equilibrium.

57
00:06:30,236 --> 00:06:37,316
In the bullish model, the manipulation phase usually trades below the opening price before the expansion higher.

58
00:06:37,736 --> 00:06:44,912
So some ICT traders prefer to look for bullish setups only while price is below the open, on a bullish day.

59
00:06:45,332 --> 00:06:50,996
In the bearish model, the reverse applies. The false move trades above the open first.

60
00:06:51,416 --> 00:06:57,632
This gives a simple location filter, but it only makes sense once you already have a directional context.

61
00:06:58,052 --> 00:07:04,700
Price location versus the open is one input to your interpretation. It is never a signal on its own.

62
00:07:06,520 --> 00:07:15,016
PO3 can be read on different timeframes. On the left, the entire intraday sequence is aggregated into one candle.

63
00:07:15,436 --> 00:07:22,612
Its open is the open, its low wick is the manipulation, and its close near the high reflects the expansion.

64
00:07:23,032 --> 00:07:30,544
This is the classic daily PO3 picture: open, low, high, close for a bullish day.

65
00:07:30,964 --> 00:07:35,356
The same idea can be applied to a session, or to an intraday swing.

66
00:07:35,776 --> 00:07:44,272
But a small PO3 inside a larger move is not necessarily part of the same market cycle. Always state which timeframe you are reading.

67
00:07:46,092 --> 00:07:53,796
Let’s close the concept with common mistakes. One: assuming every day follows PO3. Many days do not.

68
00:07:54,216 --> 00:07:58,488
Two: forcing the three phases onto a chart after the fact.

69
00:07:58,908 --> 00:08:04,788
Three: treating every breakout as manipulation. Many breakouts simply continue.

70
00:08:05,208 --> 00:08:09,984
Four: assuming distribution must reach a particular liquidity target.

71
00:08:10,404 --> 00:08:15,780
Five: confusing the opening price with equilibrium. They are different references.

72
00:08:16,200 --> 00:08:19,488
Six: ignoring market structure from Level Two.

73
00:08:19,908 --> 00:08:24,348
Seven: confusing PO3 with the Wyckoff market cycle.

74
00:08:24,768 --> 00:08:29,304
And eight: claiming institutional manipulation without evidence.

75
00:08:31,124 --> 00:08:39,332
To summarise. PO3 is a conceptual model with three phases: accumulation, manipulation and distribution.

76
00:08:39,752 --> 00:08:47,648
The opening price anchors the model. Bullish models trade below the open first; bearish models trade above it first.

77
00:08:48,068 --> 00:08:52,892
A sweep is what you observe. Manipulation is how the framework interprets it.

78
00:08:53,312 --> 00:08:58,544
And when the sequence is incomplete, the professional answer is not to force it.

79
